<Project>

  <!-- The versions live in requirements/dotnet/Packages.props, with the pins for every other
       toolchain. This file exists only to point at them.

       WHY A SHIM RATHER THAN JUST MOVING THE FILE. NuGet finds central package management by
       walking UP from each project until it meets a Directory.Packages.props, so the file has to be
       at the root whatever its contents. The `DirectoryPackagesPropsPath` property does override
       that search — verified working — but it overrides it for the RESTORE only: tooling that
       writes package versions still expects the conventional location, and a Directory.Packages.props
       created at the root by an IDE would then be silently ignored rather than merged. A shim keeps
       discovery pointing where everyone expects and puts the content where it belongs.

       Verified: a version changed in the imported file is picked up, and fails a locked restore
       with NU1004 exactly as it did before the move.

       Note for tooling: `dotnet add package` writes `Version=` into the .csproj rather than adding
       a PackageVersion here — it does that with the conventional layout too, so this arrangement
       costs nothing. Under central package management that .csproj edit is an NU1008 error; move
       the version into requirements/dotnet/Packages.props and drop the attribute. -->
  <Import Project="requirements/dotnet/Packages.props" />

</Project>
